# rebuttal-v1 — the D1 rebuttal-replay policy (corpus-v1's defeasible fragment plus
# review/rebuttal defense vocabulary)
#
# The demo (examples/rebuttal-replay/, issue #62) checks a paper, then a review round, then a
# rebuttal round as three programs over ONE artifact under this ONE trusted policy. The policy is
# the fixed reviewing standard; the program grows each round, and grounded semantics re-adjudicates
# the whole graph — so a claim's status can move justified -> defeated -> justified without any
# change to the policy. That non-monotonicity at a FIXED replay identity is the demo's point.
#
# rebuttal-v1 is corpus-v1's defeasible families (1-9) verbatim, minus corpus-v1's strict Family 10
# (the ra@1 rational-arithmetic re-check and its theory — the demo carries no certificate, so the
# suite stays defeasible-only and num-literal-free, exactly like empirical-v1/v2), plus TWO defense
# contraries the review/rebuttal round needs (see the "new in rebuttal-v1" block). Every corpus-v1
# claim that avoids Family 10 checks identically under rebuttal-v1, so round 0 reproduces the
# adaptive-pruning corpus-unit verdicts (gap on the kurtosis ablation) under the corpus standard.
#
# Design notes (the defense mechanism, mirroring empirical-v2's E4 reinstatement vocabulary):
#   * `contrary not_fixed_protocol B fixed_protocol` lets a reviewer UNDERMINE a benchmark
#     argument's protocol_fixed leaf (Reviewer 1: "the protocol was not actually held fixed").
#   * `contrary protocol_confirmed B not_fixed_protocol` is declared in ONE direction only, so an
#     author leaf concluding protocol_confirmed can undermine the reviewer's not_fixed_protocol
#     leaf with no reverse edge — the unattacked defender reinstates the benchmark argument
#     (grounded reinstatement, spec §8; the E4 one-directional-contrary trick). A symmetric pair
#     would force the reverse edge and collapse the reinstatement into a contested 2-cycle.
#   * The rebut counter-attack (author undercuts the reviewer's null replication) and the reviewer
#     undercut on the measurement claim reuse EXISTING corpus-v1 exceptions
#     (null_benchmark : environment_mismatch, measurement : measurement_artifact) — no new
#     vocabulary. Defense is policy vocabulary, not a new mechanism.

policy rebuttal-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/rebuttal-replay/round0/rebuttal-v1.policy.lara examples/rebuttal-replay/round{0,1,2}/example.lara

sort S1, S2, S3, S4, S5, S6, S7, S8, S9, S10, S11, S12, S13, S14, S15, S16, S17, S18, S19, S20, S21, S22, S23, S24, S25, S26, S27, S28

con ablation_effect(S8, S9, S10, S11) : S2
con absent : S23
con apt : S13
con apt_llama2_7b : S9
con benchmark_result(S13, S16, S10, S11) : S2
con compares(S3, S4, S5, S6, S7) : S2
con correlation(S20, S21, S22, S23) : S2
con dense_baseline : S16
con e02 : S1
con e03 : S1
con e04 : S1
con e_rep : S1
con favorable : S7
con human_preference(S26, S27, S28, S7) : S2
con instances(S24, S25, S11) : S2
con kurtosis_salience : S8
con low_memory_footprint : S12
con negative : S11
con null : S7
con openllm_avg : S10
con positive : S11
con present : S23

pred assumptions_established(S14)
pred better(S3, S4, S5, S6)
pred biased_sample(S24, S25)
pred blinded(S1)
pred code_property(S18, S19)
pred confounded_ablation(S8, S9, S10)
pred confounded_comparison(S3, S4, S5, S6)
pred confounding_variable(S20, S21, S22)
pred confounds_controlled(S1)
pred contributes(S8, S9, S10)
pred correlated(S20, S21, S22)
pred covers_paths(S17, S18)
pred covers_scope(S1, S13)
pred derives(S14, S15)
pred environment_mismatch(S13, S16, S10)
pred fixed_protocol(S13, S16, S1)
pred generalizes_to(S24, S25)
pred holds(S12, S13)
pred holds_analytically(S15)
pred independent_samples(S1)
pred inspects(S17, S18, S19)
pred isolated(S8, S1)
pred matched_environment(S1)
pred matched_protocol(S1)
pred matched_setup(S3, S4, S1)
pred measurement_artifact(S12, S13)
pred not_better(S3, S4, S5, S6)
pred not_correlated(S20, S21, S22)
pred not_fixed_protocol(S13, S16, S1)
pred not_performs(S13, S16, S10)
pred observes(S1, S12, S13)
pred performs(S13, S16, S10)
pred powered(S1)
pred preferred(S26, S27, S28)
pred preregistered(S1)
pred protocol_confirmed(S13, S16, S1)
pred protocol_deviation(S13, S16, S10)
pred rater_bias(S26, S27, S28)
pred reports(S1, S2)
pred representative_sample(S1, S25)
pred scope_licensed(S15)
pred scope_matched(S3, S5, S6)
pred significance_reported(S1)
pred sufficient_raters(S1)
pred underpowered_correlation(S20, S21, S22)
pred underpowered_null(S3, S4, S5, S6)
pred unsound_assumption(S15)
pred valid_benchmark(S16, S10)
pred valid_instances(S1)
pred valid_instrument(S12, S1)
pred valid_measurement(S5, S1)
pred variance_reported(S1)
pred version_matched(S18, S17)
pred version_skew(S18)
pred within_noise(S8, S9, S10)

# --- Family 1: controlled comparison ------------------------------------------------------------

rule controlled_comparison(S, B, Q, D, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, compares(S, B, Q, D, favorable)) ]
  conclusion = better(S, B, Q, D)
  question baseline_parity      : matched_setup(S, B, Exp)   (mandatory)
  question measurement_validity : valid_measurement(Q, Exp)  (mandatory)
  question variance_reported    : variance_reported(Exp)     (mandatory)
  question scope_match          : scope_matched(S, Q, D)     (optional)

rule null_comparison(S, B, Q, D, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, compares(S, B, Q, D, null)) ]
  conclusion = not_better(S, B, Q, D)
  question baseline_parity      : matched_setup(S, B, Exp)   (mandatory)
  question measurement_validity : valid_measurement(Q, Exp)  (mandatory)
  question adequate_power       : powered(Exp)               (mandatory)

# --- Family 2: intervention / ablation ----------------------------------------------------------

rule component_ablation(C, S, Q, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, ablation_effect(C, S, Q, positive)) ]
  conclusion = contributes(C, S, Q)
  question single_variable   : isolated(C, Exp)        (mandatory)
  question protocol_parity   : matched_protocol(Exp)   (mandatory)
  question variance_reported : variance_reported(Exp)  (mandatory)

# --- Family 3: observation / measurement --------------------------------------------------------

rule measurement(P, S, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ observes(Exp, P, S) ]
  conclusion = holds(P, S)
  question instrument_validity : valid_instrument(P, Exp)     (mandatory)
  question coverage            : covers_scope(Exp, S)         (mandatory)
  question confound_control    : confounds_controlled(Exp)    (optional)

# --- Family 4: analytic -------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule analytic_argument(P, Prf)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ derives(Prf, P) ]
  conclusion = holds_analytically(P)
  question assumptions_hold : assumptions_established(Prf)  (mandatory)
  question scope_licensed   : scope_licensed(P)             (mandatory)

# --- Family 5: benchmark / stress evaluation ----------------------------------------------------

rule benchmark_evaluation(S, B, Q, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, benchmark_result(S, B, Q, positive)) ]
  conclusion = performs(S, B, Q)
  question protocol_fixed    : fixed_protocol(S, B, Exp)   (mandatory)
  question benchmark_valid   : valid_benchmark(B, Q)       (mandatory)
  question environment_match : matched_environment(Exp)    (optional)

rule null_benchmark(S, B, Q, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, benchmark_result(S, B, Q, negative)) ]
  conclusion = not_performs(S, B, Q)
  question protocol_fixed    : fixed_protocol(S, B, Exp)   (mandatory)
  question environment_match : matched_environment(Exp)    (mandatory)

# --- Family 6: code inspection ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule code_inspection(P, Src, Insp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ inspects(Insp, Src, P) ]
  conclusion = code_property(Src, P)
  question version_match : version_matched(Src, Insp)  (mandatory)
  question path_coverage : covers_paths(Insp, Src)     (mandatory)

# --- Family 7: statistical correlation ----------------------------------------------------------

rule statistical_correlation(X, Y, S, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, correlation(X, Y, S, present)) ]
  conclusion = correlated(X, Y, S)
  question significance     : significance_reported(Exp)  (mandatory)
  question independence     : independent_samples(Exp)    (mandatory)
  question confound_control : confounds_controlled(Exp)   (optional)

rule null_correlation(X, Y, S, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, correlation(X, Y, S, absent)) ]
  conclusion = not_correlated(X, Y, S)
  question significance   : significance_reported(Exp)  (mandatory)
  question adequate_power : powered(Exp)                (mandatory)

# --- Family 8: inductive generalization ---------------------------------------------------------

rule inductive_generalization(P, C, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, instances(P, C, positive)) ]
  conclusion = generalizes_to(P, C)
  question sample_representative : representative_sample(Exp, C)  (mandatory)
  question instance_validity     : valid_instances(Exp)           (mandatory)

# --- Family 9: blind paired human evaluation ----------------------------------------------------

rule human_evaluation(S, B, Q, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, human_preference(S, B, Q, favorable)) ]
  conclusion = preferred(S, B, Q)
  question blinding        : blinded(Exp)            (mandatory)
  question rater_power     : sufficient_raters(Exp)  (mandatory)
  question preregistration : preregistered(Exp)      (optional)

# --- Conflict (corpus-v1) -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Symmetric pairs only where the sample concludes both sides through the dual rules above. A unit
# with args on both sides of one pair must declare the mutual rebut edges (attack completeness).

contrary better(S, B, Q, D)     not_better(S, B, Q, D)
contrary not_better(S, B, Q, D) better(S, B, Q, D)

contrary performs(S, B, Q)     not_performs(S, B, Q)
contrary not_performs(S, B, Q) performs(S, B, Q)

contrary correlated(X, Y, S)     not_correlated(X, Y, S)
contrary not_correlated(X, Y, S) correlated(X, Y, S)

# --- Conflict (new in rebuttal-v1) --------------------------------------------------------------

# A reviewer UNDERMINE on a benchmark argument's protocol_fixed leaf: the reviewer finds the
# protocol was not held fixed (hyperparameters tuned on the eval tasks). Declared so an arg
# concluding not_fixed_protocol attacks the leaf that discharges benchmark_evaluation's
# protocol_fixed CQ.
contrary not_fixed_protocol(S, B, Exp) fixed_protocol(S, B, Exp)

# The author's REBUTTAL counter-undermine: a leaf concluding protocol_confirmed defeats the
# reviewer's not_fixed_protocol leaf. ONE direction only (see header): nothing licenses the
# reviewer's finding to attack the author's confirmation back, so the unattacked defender
# reinstates the benchmark argument instead of forming a contested 2-cycle.
contrary protocol_confirmed(S, B, Exp) not_fixed_protocol(S, B, Exp)

# --- Exceptions (undercutting conditions), at least one per rule (component_ablation carries two) --

exception controlled_comparison    : confounded_comparison(S, B, Q, D)
exception null_comparison          : underpowered_null(S, B, Q, D)
exception component_ablation       : confounded_ablation(C, S, Q)
exception component_ablation       : within_noise(C, S, Q)
exception measurement              : measurement_artifact(P, S)
exception analytic_argument        : unsound_assumption(P)
exception benchmark_evaluation     : protocol_deviation(S, B, Q)
exception null_benchmark           : environment_mismatch(S, B, Q)
exception code_inspection          : version_skew(Src)
exception statistical_correlation  : confounding_variable(X, Y, S)
exception null_correlation         : underpowered_correlation(X, Y, S)
exception inductive_generalization : biased_sample(P, C)
exception human_evaluation         : rater_bias(S, B, Q)

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): every rule here is defeasible (rebuttal-v1 drops corpus-v1's
# sole strict rule), so no proposition in any contrary pair — including the two new defense pairs —
# is strict-reachable, and the policy passes the R12 check exactly as empirical-v1/v2 do.
